title: primeval_context
---
// The primeval trail's people (the jungle dimension): the ranger at the
// trailhead by the gate, the paleontologist at the tar pits past the end of
// the trail. One node per resident, named `primeval_<resident>`.
//
// Everyday talk only (Jon, 2026-10-29: "we are world building not quest
// building"): no flags, no errands.
//
// Never played. No placement names this node; it declares the zone.
===

title: primeval_brandvold
---
Ranger Brandvold: Welcome to the primeval trail. Stay on the path, carry water, and do not make eye contact with the chickens.
-> The chickens?
    Ranger Brandvold: Everybody worries about the raptors. The raptors are honest. The chickens know what they used to be.
-> What's at the end of the trail?
    Ranger Brandvold: The canopy, then the tar pits, then Feld's dig. Past that, nobody has filled in a form.
    Ranger Brandvold: There is also a temple with a king in it. I do not patrol the temple. The temple patrols me.
-> Is the trail safe?
    Ranger Brandvold: We marked it last month. Check that the marker is still attached to a tree before you follow it. A triceratops carried one off last week.
-> Thanks, ranger.
    Ranger Brandvold: Sign back in when you return. Dlamini sends me out to look for anyone who forgets.
===

title: primeval_feld
---
Dr Feld: Don't step there. Or there. Fine, step there, but gently. That is a layer.
-> What are you digging for?
    Dr Feld: Fossils. The tar keeps everything: mammoths, sabre-tooths, a lunchbox I think was mine.
    Dr Feld: The difficulty is that most of my specimens are still alive and running past the tent.
-> Isn't it dangerous?
    Dr Feld: It can be. A triceratops sat on my survey grid on Tuesday.
    Dr Feld: I have recorded it as a sedimentary event.
-> What's the mammoth doing here?
    Dr Feld: Sinking, very slowly, for about thirty thousand years. People walk along its back. I have stopped asking them not to.
-> Do you ever get homesick?
    Dr Feld: I miss being able to finish a cup of tea. At home the biggest thing that could steal it was the dog.
-> Good luck with the dig.
    Dr Feld: Thank you. Tell Dlamini I'll be here until dark.
===

title: primeval_tock
---
Professor Tock: Shh. Not you, the sky. I'm listening for it.
-> Listening for what?
    Professor Tock: The meteor. The big one. Everything down there has been waiting for it for sixty-six million years, they just don't know it.
    Professor Tock: I have the telescope pointed at the eastern horizon. It's very cold up here at four in the morning. The institute did send me a thermos.
-> Isn't the volcano dangerous?
    Professor Tock: Yes, but I've got the telescope on the ridge. I won't go down to the crater with the rest of them.
-> What if it never comes?
    Professor Tock: Then perhaps I can get the institute to pay for another year of observations. I've been recording the sky every night.
-> What do you do when it is cloudy?
    Professor Tock: Keep the time. A gap in the book would look like I had slept through the end of the world.
-> Enjoy the view.
    Professor Tock: Wave if you see anything on fire coming down. Not the lava. The lava's always on fire.
===

title: primeval_twitchell
---
Mildred Twitchell: Shh. Shh! Get down. You'll put up the swallows.
-> What are you watching?
    Mildred Twitchell: The rookery. Swallows, a shoebill, and penguins. Penguins! In a jungle, beside a volcano, a long way from any ice.
    Mildred Twitchell: I stopped asking why in the first week. Now I just count them. There are far too many.
-> Are the swallows African or European?
    Mildred Twitchell: African. Unladen. I've timed them. Please don't ask me how fast; people always do, and then they laugh.
-> What is the shoebill doing?
    Mildred Twitchell: Standing still. It has been standing still since Tuesday. It is the most frightening bird I have ever seen, and I have seen a cassowary angry.
-> Have the penguins built nests?
    Mildred Twitchell: Yes! One of them took a pebble from my boot. I went all the way back to the station to find another one. I did not want to be responsible for a divorce.
-> I'll leave you to it.
    Mildred Twitchell: Go round behind me, please. The shoebill's just moved.
===

title: primeval_stratton
---
Dr Petra Stratton: Mind the beds. Everything down here is in the right order, which is more than I can say for upstairs.
-> What is wrong with upstairs?
    Dr Petra Stratton: The dinosaurs are in it. Look at the board. They belong down here, sixty-six million years down, in the layer with the ash. That layer. The grey one.
    Dr Petra Stratton: Every morning I cross them out of the top layer, and every morning one of them walks over the hole.
-> What are you digging?
    Dr Petra Stratton: The skull. Long-necked chap along the seam beside it. Feld at the tar pits says hers is older. Hers is in tar. Tar is not a layer, it is a mess.
-> Isn't a living dinosaur good evidence?
    Dr Petra Stratton: It's wonderful to have a live specimen, if you can keep it in view. It doesn't tell me when these beds were laid down. I need samples from the seam.
-> How do you know which way is older?
    Dr Petra Stratton: In an undisturbed bed, the older sediment is underneath. Then the earth folds it, faults it, or turns the whole lot over. So I look at the structures in the rock before I trust my compass.
-> I'll leave you to it.
    Dr Petra Stratton: Mind the compys at the east end. They've been getting into the sample bags.
===

title: primeval_dlamini
---
Ranger Dlamini: (into the radio) Station to Ridge, the walker's left the station. Over. (to you) Sorry. Welcome to the ranger station. Don't touch the jars.
-> What do you do here?
    Ranger Dlamini: I keep the radio. Brandvold walks the trail, Feld digs, Tock watches the sky, Twitchell counts the penguins. Every one of them checks in. I know where every walker on the trail is.
    Ranger Dlamini: (into the radio) Feld, station. Have you got your radio on? Over.
-> What is in the jars?
    Ranger Dlamini: Ferns, eggs, and compys we've rescued from tar. They are very small and very annoyed and they bite through the lids. I need better lids before the next batch hatches.
-> What is in the lost-property box?
    Ranger Dlamini: One boot, a monocle, an umbrella. All from the same walker. We never found the rest of him. He sent a postcard from the mountain, so I think he's fine.
-> I'll be on my way.
    Ranger Dlamini: Check in at the ridge. Brandvold needs to know which part of the trail you're on.
===
